The error **"Methods annotated with '@Transactional' must be overridable"** occurs because Spring uses CGLIB proxies to handle transactions. For Spring to "wrap" your method in a transaction, the method must be **public** and the class must not be final.
However, there is a **hidden architectural bug** here: In Spring, calling a @Transactional method from within the same class (e.g., this.processChunkRaw(...)) **bypasses the proxy**. This means your "mini-transactions" aren't actually starting, which is exactly why your database is locking up and counts are getting corrupted.
### The Next Bug: Transactional Proxy Bypass & MDC Context Loss
 1. **The Proxy Bug:** Because executeAsyncBatchCreation calls processChunkRaw directly, Spring never sees the @Transactional annotation on the chunk. For 500,000 records, this causes the database to treat the whole process as one giant, unstable transaction or a series of auto-commits that can't roll back.
 2. **The MDC Bug:** MDC (Mapped Diagnostic Context) is ThreadLocal. When you use CompletableFuture.runAsync, the worker thread **loses the batchId and user info**. Your logs for 500,000 rows will be "silent," making it impossible to debug which chunk failed.
### File: JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
To solve this, we will use **Self-Injection**. This is a professional pattern that forces the call through the Spring Proxy so that @Transactional and MDC are respected.
#### 1. Add Self-Injection and Update executeAsyncBatchCreation
Add this at the top of your class and update the loop logic.
```java
@Autowired
@Lazy
private JournalRequestServiceImpl self; // Self-injection to solve the Proxy Bypass bug

@Override
@Async("bulkExecutor")
public void executeAsyncBatchCreation(String batchId, String requestId, String commonRemarks, String creatorId, Integer creatorRole) {
    MDC.put("batchId", batchId);
    MDC.put("user", creatorId);
    
    // Capture MDC context to pass to worker threads
    Map<String, String> contextMap = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();

    try {
        jdbcTemplate.update("DELETE FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", batchId);
        List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> cachedRows = journalBulkValidationService.getValidRowsFromCache(requestId);
        
        int totalRows = cachedRows.size();
        int batchSize = 5000;
        LongAdder totalDebit = new LongAdder();
        LongAdder totalCredit = new LongAdder();
        List<CompletableFuture<Void>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < totalRows; i += batchSize) {
            final int startIdx = i;
            final int endIdx = Math.min(i + batchSize, totalRows);
            final List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> chunk = new ArrayList<>(cachedRows.subList(startIdx, endIdx));

            // USE 'self' instead of 'this' to ensure @Transactional works
            CompletableFuture<Void> future = CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
                // Restore MDC context in the worker thread
                if (contextMap != null) MDC.setContextMap(contextMap);
                try {
                    self.processChunkRaw(chunk, batchId, commonRemarks, creatorId, creatorRole, startIdx, totalDebit, totalCredit);
                } finally {
                    MDC.clear();
                }
            }, chunkWorkerExecutor);
            
            futures.add(future);
        }

        CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).get(45, TimeUnit.MINUTES);

        // ... Summary insertion logic (already fixed in previous step) ...

    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("BATCH_CRITICAL_FAILURE | Error: {}", e.getMessage());
        handleAsyncFailure(batchId, creatorId, requestId, e.getMessage());
    } finally {
        MDC.clear();
    }
}

